## Separate transmission authority

`WorkAuthorization.model_transmission` is an immutable authorization field, bound
in issuance/recovery evidence and in the revision-4 profile/launch record.
`adapter/model_transmission.py` enforces it in `ResponsesReasoning.run` before the
initial payload and when building every tool-result continuation. The dispatcher
still returns its full governed local result to the controller; local readability
is independent of transmission eligibility.

The production policy is **default DENY**. Initial reasoning requires separate
exact-payload SHA-256 clearance and an authority reference. File transmission
requires an exact canonical path, hash of the actual returned content, an explicit
category/authority reference, current read authorization, and sensitive-class
checks. Changes and truncated reads do not inherit clearance. Protected governing
and evidence categories require their corresponding explicit clearance; credential,
private and hidden-path classes cannot be cleared through ordinary-file permission.
No secret scanner is claimed: exact-content classification/clearance is a trusted
controller/Architect input, never inferred from readability or generated by the
Programmer. No actual E1 content has been independently cleared in this candidate.

Lists and searches, including paths, names, matches and truncation metadata, are
not transmitted under the current policy. Other tool-result bodies, error text,
hashes, status/evidence, finish/expansion echoes and execution output are withheld.
Uncleared results receive the same constant redaction indication, including across
SUCCEEDED/DENIED/INDETERMINATE. This avoids an outcome-predicate channel over
protected execution inputs. The full result and redaction decision remain in the
controller audit. No new tool, MCP/plugin, generic shell/Git, or payload network
authority is introduced. Model-originated continuation is preserved; it supplies
no new permission for controller-derived information.

The exact endpoint/model/transport remains unchanged: OpenAI Responses at
